* 1.6.1 - Uses chain-gang for signing
* 2.0.0 - First Public release
* 2.1.0 - Renamed to financing-service, configurable UTXO refresh period, CI build and test
* 2.2.0 - Secrets resolved from environment, per-client and admin API keys, per-IP rate limiting, concurrent funding, system requirements document
* 3.0.0 - Breaking: HTTP/2 no longer served (actix-web http2 feature removed to drop the h2 0.3 crate flagged by RUSTSEC-2026-0258) and SR-NFR-006 withdrawn. Also: optional OpenTelemetry trace export via OTLP, dependency updates including OpenTelemetry 0.32 and chain-gang 0.10.1 from crates.io, telemetry startup fix, rotated test key
* 4.0.0 - Breaking: error responses now use a status mapped to their kind (400, 404, 409, 500, 502, 503) instead of 422 for everything; partial_broadcast alone keeps 422. Also: machine-readable error codes on every error response, optional idempotency_key on POST /fund, a locking script per outpoint, each outpoint's value and script reported in the fund response, and the interface/network matrix documented
* 4.1.0 - regtest support: a new `rpc` blockchain interface talking directly to a node, the only one that reaches regtest, with each client's funding address imported into the node's wallet automatically at startup and when a client is added at runtime; chain-gang 0.11.1
* 4.2.0 - optional mapi-lite transaction broadcaster: with a `[mapi_lite]` section funding transactions are submitted to a mapi-lite server instead of the blockchain interface, and `GET /status` reports the broadcaster in use. Also: a new `GET /ready` readiness endpoint, which probes mapi-lite and answers 503 when it is unreachable, leaving `GET /health` a liveness check that no upstream can fail so an outage cannot restart the container; two new `POST /fund` error codes, `broadcast_outcome_unknown` (504) when a submit's fate cannot be determined, whose inputs are then held back rather than offered to the next funding request, and `broadcast_rejected` (409) when the upstream refuses a transaction it will not accept on retry; chain-gang 0.11.2 (bounded allocation when parsing transactions)
* 4.3.0 - Funding correctness, and the fee rate made configurable. **Read `index` in the `POST /fund` response rather than assuming 1:** a funding transaction whose change would be dust no longer carries a change output, so its funded outputs start at 0, and funding the reported maximum always takes that form. Fees are no longer hardcoded: a `[fees]` section sets `satoshis_per_kb`, defaulting to 100, where the superseded expression charged 750 for an ordinary transaction -- so an upgrade without that section pays less, and picks different UTXOs, because a cheaper transaction can be funded by a smaller input. With `[mapi_lite]` configured the rate comes from its fee quote instead, falling back to the configured number when one cannot be had. `GET /balance` reports `max_fundable`, the largest amount a single `POST /fund` can request, and a refusal now distinguishes `insufficient_balance` from `no_suitable_utxo` -- the wallet not holding enough, against holding enough in a shape it cannot spend -- each quoting a figure the caller can act on rather than a single-input estimate that read as "1230 available, 1230 required". The reported balance is derived from the unspent set rather than queried separately, so it cannot disagree with what is spendable, and unconfirmed outputs are eligible for funding. An outpoint the service has spent is held back until the chain reports it, and the change it created held in the cache until then, so a refresh during the gap can no longer hand the same outpoint to the next request; a rejection naming a conflicting transaction is treated as final and reported as `broadcast_rejected` whatever the upstream's retryable flag says, because a conflict is in the inputs and resubmitting cannot help. Cached chain state is reused rather than refetched while it is younger than `service.chain_state_max_age_seconds`, and a refused or uncertain broadcast marks it stale. Outbound requests are bounded by `blockchain_interface.max_requests_per_second`, 3 by default for `woc`; for `woc` that bounds HTTP requests, applied inside chain-gang, because one interface call became many requests when paging arrived. chain-gang 0.11.4: WhatsOnChain UTXO reads follow every page, where `/unspent` stopped at 1000 entries and returned a silently truncated set -- 1000 of 20960 on a busy mainnet address, understating spendable funds by the same margin -- and an address holding an unconfirmed output can be read at all. Also: CI runners pinned off `ubuntu-latest` and moved deliberately to 26.04
* 4.3.1 - A restart no longer hands out outpoints the service has just spent. The inputs a funding transaction spent, and the change it created, were held back only in memory until the chain reported them, so a restart forgot them; the chain it came back to had usually not seen those transactions yet, and the service rebuilt them byte for byte and gave each outpoint to a second caller. That state is now written to disk after every funding commit and read back at startup, so a restarted service carries on from its own unconfirmed change. **The new file must survive a restart**: it sits beside `dynamic_config.filename` as `dynamic.inflight.json`, so a deployment that already keeps `dynamic.toml` across restarts keeps it too; `service.inflight_state_file` overrides the location. Without it the service behaves as 4.3.0 did. Also chain-gang 0.11.5: WhatsOnChain goes on listing an output after a mempool transaction spends it, flagged `isSpentInMempoolTx`, and earlier releases reported such outputs as unspent; they are now left out, so a transaction still unconfirmed after its ten-minute reservation does not have its input offered again
